         order if `a` is Fortran *contiguous* in memory, C-like order\n225 |         otherwise.\n226 | \n227 |     Returns\n228 |     -------\n229 |     reshaped_array : ndarray\n230 |         This will be a new view object if possible; otherwise, it will\n231 |         be a copy.  Note there is no guarantee of the *memory layout* (C- or\n232 |         Fortran- contiguous) of the returned array.\n233 | \n234 |     See Also\n235 |     --------\n236 |     ndarray.reshape : Equivalent method.\n237 | \n238 |     Notes\n239 |     -----\n240 |     It is not always possible to change the shape of an array without\n241 |     copying the data. If you want an error to be raised when the data is copied,\n242 |     you should assign the new shape to the shape attribute of the array::\n243 | \n244 |      >>> a = np.zeros((10, 2))\n245 | \n246 |      # A transpose makes the array non-contiguous\n247 |      >>> b = a.T\n248 | \n249 |      # Taking a view makes it possible to modify the shape without modifying\n250 |      # the initial object.\n251 |      >>> c = b.view()\n252 |      >>> c.shape = (20)\n253 |      Traceback (most recent call last):\n254 |         ...\n255 |      AttributeError: Incompatible shape for in-place modification. Use\n256 |      `.reshape()` to make a copy with the desired shape.\n257 | \n258 |     The `order` keyword gives the index ordering both for *fetching* the values\n259 |     from `a`, and then *placing* the values into the output array.\n260 |     For example, let's say you have an array:\n261 | \n262 |     >>> a = np.arange(6).reshape((3, 2))\n263 |     >>> a\n264 |     array([[0, 1],\n265 |            [2, 3],\n266 |            [4, 5]])\n267 | \n268 |     You can think of reshaping as first raveling the array (using the given\n269 |     index order), then inserting the elements from the raveled array into the\n270 |     new array using the same kind of index ordering as was used for the\n271 |     raveling.\n272 | \n273 |     >>> np.reshape(a, (2, 3)) # C-like index ordering\n274 |     array([[0, 1, 2],\n275 |            [3, 4, 5]])\n276 |     >>> np.reshape(np.ravel(a), (2, 3)) # equivalent to C ravel then C reshape\n277 |     array([[0, 1, 2],\n278 |            [3, 4, 5]])\n279 |     >>> np.reshape(a, (2, 3), order='F') # Fortran-like index ordering\n280 |     array([[0, 4, 3],\n281 |            [2, 1, 5]])\n282 |     >>> np.reshape(np.ravel(a, order='F'), (2, 3), order='F')\n283 |     array([[0, 4, 3],\n284 |            [2, 1, 5]])\n285 | \n286 |     Examples\n287 |     --------\n288 |     >>> a = np.array([[1,2,3], [4,5,6]])\n289 |     >>> np.reshape(a, 6)\n290 |     array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])\n291 |     >>> np.reshape(a, 6, order='F')\n292 |     array([1, 4, 2, 5, 3, 6])\n293 | \n294 |     >>> np.reshape(a, (3,-1))       # the unspecified value is inferred to be 2\n295 |     array([[1, 2],\n296 |            [3, 4],\n297 |            [5, 6]])\n298 |     \"\"\"\n299 |     return _wrapfunc(a, 'reshape', newshape, order=order)\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/ma/core.py:MaskedArray.reshape`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `reshape`.\n```\n4587 | def reshape(self, *s, **kwargs):\n4588 |         \"\"\"\n4589 |         Give a new shape to the array without changing its data.\n4590 | \n4591 |         Returns a masked array containing the same data, but with a new shape.\n4592 |         The result is a view on the original array; if this is not possible, a\n4593 |         ValueError is raised.\n4594 | \n4595 |         Parameters\n4596 |         ----------\n4597 |         shape : int or tuple of ints\n4598 |             The new shape should be compatible with the original shape. If an\n4599 |             integer is supplied, then the result will be a 1-D array of that\n4600 |             length.\n4601 |         order : {'C', 'F'}, optional\n4602 |             Determines whether the array data should be viewed as in C\n4603 |             (row-major) or FORTRAN (column-major) order.\n4604 | \n4605 |         Returns\n4606 |         -------\n4607 |         reshaped_array : array\n4608 |             A new view on the array.\n4609 | \n4610 |         See Also\n4611 |         --------\n4612 |         reshape : Equivalent function in the masked array module.\n4613 |         numpy.ndarray.reshape : Equivalent method on ndarray object.\n4614 |         numpy.reshape : Equivalent function in the NumPy module.\n4615 | \n4616 |         Notes\n4617 |         -----\n4618 |         The reshaping operation cannot guarantee that a copy will not be made,\n4619 |         to modify the shape in place, use ``a.shape = s``\n4620 | \n4621 |         Examples\n4622 |         --------\n4623 |         >>> x = np.ma.array([[1,2],[3,4]], mask=[1,0,0,1])\n4624 |         >>> x\n4625 |         masked_array(\n4626 |           data=[[--, 2],\n4627 |                 [3, --]],\n4628 |           mask=[[ True, False],\n4629 |                 [False,  True]],\n4630 |           fill_value=999999)\n4631 |         >>> x = x.reshape((4,1))\n4632 |         >>> x\n4633 |         masked_array(\n4634 |           data=[[--],\n4635 |                 [2],\n4636 |                 [3],\n4637 |                 [--]],\n4638 |           mask=[[ True],\n4639 |                 [False],\n4640 |                 [False],\n4641 |                 [ True]],\n4642 |           fill_value=999999)\n4643 | \n4644 |         \"\"\"\n4645 |         kwargs.update(order=kwargs.get('order', 'C'))\n4646 |         result = self._data.reshape(*s, **kwargs).view(type(self))\n4647 |         result._update_from(self)\n4648 |         mask = self._mask\n4649 |         if mask is not nomask:\n4650 |             result._mask = mask.reshape(*s, **kwargs)\n4651 |         return result\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/ma/core.py:reshape`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `reshape`.\n```\n7137 | def reshape(a, new_shape, order='C'):\n7138 |     \"\"\"\n7139 |     Returns an array containing the same data with a new shape.\n7140 | \n7141 |     Refer to `Maske