n'\n2849 |         The input samples.\n2850 |         If `'no_validation'`, no validation is performed on `X`. This is\n2851 |         useful for meta-estimator which can delegate input validation to\n2852 |         their underlying estimator(s). In that case `y` must be passed and\n2853 |         the only accepted `check_params` are `multi_output` and\n2854 |         `y_numeric`.\n2855 | \n2856 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default='no_validation'\n2857 |         The targets.\n2858 | \n2859 |         - If `None`, :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array` is called on `X`. If\n2860 |           the estimator's `requires_y` tag is True, then an error will be raised.\n2861 |         - If `'no_validation'`, :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array` is called\n2862 |           on `X` and the estimator's `requires_y` tag is ignored. This is a default\n2863 |           placeholder and is never meant to be explicitly set. In that case `X` must be\n2864 |           passed.\n2865 |         - Otherwise, only `y` with `_check_y` or both `X` and `y` are checked with\n2866 |           either :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array` or\n2867 |           :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_X_y` depending on `validate_separately`.\n2868 | \n2869 |     reset : bool, default=True\n2870 |         Whether to reset the `n_features_in_` attribute.\n2871 |         If False, the input will be checked for consistency with data\n2872 |         provided when reset was last True.\n2873 | \n2874 |         .. note::\n2875 | \n2876 |            It is recommended to call `reset=True` in `fit` and in the first\n2877 |            call to `partial_fit`. All other methods that validate `X`\n2878 |            should set `reset=False`.\n2879 | \n2880 |     validate_separately : False or tuple of dicts, default=False\n2881 |         Only used if `y` is not `None`.\n2882 |         If `False`, call :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_X_y`. Else, it must be a tuple of\n2883 |         kwargs to be used for calling :func:`~sklearn.