tuple (*nx*, *ny*), the number of hexagons\n5011 |             in the *x*-direction and the *y*-direction. In the\n5012 |             *y*-direction, counting is done along vertically aligned\n5013 |             hexagons, not along the zig-zag chains of hexagons; see the\n5014 |             following illustration.\n5015 | \n5016 |             .. plot::\n5017 | \n5018 |                import numpy\n5019 |                import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n5020 | \n5021 |                np.random.seed(19680801)\n5022 |                n= 300\n5023 |                x = np.random.standard_normal(n)\n5024 |                y = np.random.standard_normal(n)\n5025 | \n5026 |                fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(4, 4))\n5027 |                h = ax.hexbin(x, y, gridsize=(5, 3))\n5028 |                hx, hy = h.get_offsets().T\n5029 |                ax.plot(hx[24::3], hy[24::3], 'ro-')\n5030 |                ax.plot(hx[-3:], hy[-3:], 'ro-')\n5031 |                ax.set_title('gridsize=(5, 3)')\n5032 |                ax.axis('off')\n5033 | \n5034 |             To get approximately regular hexagons, choose\n5035 |             :math:`n_x = \\\\sqrt{3}\\\\,n_y`.\n5036 | \n5037 |         bins : 'log' or int or sequence, default: None\n5038 |             Discretization of the hexagon values.\n5039 | \n5040 |             - If *None*, no binning is applied; the color of each hexagon\n5041 |               directly corresponds to its count value.\n5042 |             - If 'log', use a logarithmic scale for the colormap.\n5043 |               Internally, :math:`log_{10}(i+1)` is used to determine the\n5044 |               hexagon color. This is equivalent to ``norm=LogNorm()``.\n5045 |             - If an integer, divide the counts in the specified number\n5046 |               of bins, and color the hexagons accordingly.\n5047 |             - If a sequence of values, the values of the lower bound of\n5048 |               the bins to be used.\n5049 | \n5050 |         xscale : {'linear', 'log'}, default: 'linear'\n5051 |             Use a linear or log10 scale on the horizontal axis.\n5052 | \n5053 |         yscale : {'linear', 'log'}, default: 'linear'\n5054 |             Use a linear or log10 scale on the vertical axis.\n5055 | \n5056 |         mincnt : int >= 0, default: *None*\n5057 |             If not *None*, only display cells with at least *mincnt*\n5058 |             number of points in the cell.\n5059 | \n5060 |         marginals : bool, default: *False*\n5061 |             If marginals is *True*, plot the marginal density as\n5062 |             colormapped rectangles along the bottom of the x-axis and\n5063 |             left of the y-axis.\n5064 | \n5065 |         extent : 4-tuple of float, default: *None*\n5066 |             The limits of the bins (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax).\n5067 |             The default assigns the limits based on\n5068 |             *gridsize*, *x*, *y*, *xscale* and *yscale*.\n5069 | \n5070 |             If *xscale* or *yscale* is set to 'log', the limits are\n5071 |             expected to be the exponent for a power of 10. E.g. for\n5072 |             x-limits of 1 and 50 in 'linear' scale and y-limits\n5073 |             of 10 and 1000 in 'log' scale, enter (1, 50, 1, 3).\n5074 | \n5075 |         Returns\n5076 |         -------\n5077 |         `~matplotlib.collections.PolyCollection`\n5078 |             A `.PolyCollection` defining the hexagonal bins.\n5079 | \n5080 |             - `.PolyCollection.get_offsets` contains a Mx2 array containing\n5081 |               the x, y positions of the M hexagon centers in data coordinates.\n5082 |             - `.PolyCollection.get_array` contains the values of the M\n5083 |               hexagons.\n5084 | \n5085 |             If *marginals* is *True*, horizontal\n5086 |             bar and vertical bar (both PolyCollections) will be attached\n5087 |             to the return collection as attributes *hbar* and *vbar*.\n5088 | \n5089 |         Other Parameters\n5090 |         ----------------\n5091 |         %(cmap_doc)s\n5092 | \n5093 |         %(norm_doc)s\n5094 | \n5095 |         %(vmin_vmax_doc)s\n5096 | \n5097 |         alpha : float between 0 and 1, optional\n5098 |             The alpha blending value, between 0 (transparent) and 1 (opaque).\n5099 | \n5100 |         linewidths : float, default: *None*\n5101 |             If *None*, defaults to :rc:`patch.linewidth`.\n5102 | \n5103 |         edgecolors : {'face', 'none', *None*} or color, default: 'face'\n5104 |             The color of the hexagon edges. Possible values are:\n5105 | \n5106 |             - 'face': Draw the edges in the same color as the fill color.\n5107 |             - 'none': No edges are drawn. This can sometimes lead to unsightly\n5108 |               unpainted pixels between the hexagons.\n5109 |             - *None*: Draw outlines in the default color.\n5110 |             - An explicit color.\n5111 | \n5112 |         reduce_C_function : callable, default: `numpy.mean`\n5113 |             The function to aggregate *C* within the bins. It is ignored if\n5114 |             *C* is not given. This must have the signature::\n5115 | \n5116 |                 def reduce_C_function(C: array) -> float\n5117 | \n5118 |             Commonly used functions are:\n5119 | \n5120 |             - `numpy.mean`: average of the points\n5121 |             - `numpy.sum`: integral of the point values\n5122 |             - `numpy.amax`: value taken from the largest point\n5123 | \n5124 |             By default will only reduce cells with at least 1 point because some\n5125 |             reduction functions (such as `numpy.amax`) will error/warn with empty\n5126 |             input. Changing *mincnt* will adjust the cutoff, and if set to 0 will\n5127 |             pass empty input to the reduction function.\n5128 | \n5129 |         %(colorizer_doc)s\n5130 | \n5131 |         data : indexable object, optional\n5132 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n5133 | \n5134 |         **kwargs : `~matplotlib.collections.PolyCollection` properties\n5135 |             All other keyword arguments are passed on to `.PolyCollection`:\n5136 | \n5137 |             %(PolyCollection:kwdoc)s\n5138 | \n5139 |         See Also\n5140 |         --------\n5141 |         hist2d : 2D histogram rectangular bins\n5142 |         \"\"\"\n5143 |         self._process_unit_info([(\"x\", x), (\"y\", y)], kwargs, convert=False)\n5144 | \n5145 |         x, y, C = cbook.delete_masked_points(x, y, C)\n5146 | \n5147 |         # Set the size of the hexagon grid\n5148 |         if np.iterable(gridsize):\n5149 |             nx, ny = gridsize\n5150 |         else:\n5151 |             nx = gridsize\n5152 |             ny = int(nx / math.sqrt(3))\n5153 |         # Count the number of data in each hexagon\n5154 |         x = np.asarray(x, float)\n5155 |         y = np.asarray(y, float)\n5156 | \n5157 |         # Will be log()'d if necessary, and then rescaled.\n5158 |         tx = x\n5159 |         ty = y\n5160 | \n5161 |         if xscale == 'log':\n5162 |             if np.any(x <= 0.0):\n5163 |                 raise ValueError(\n5164 |                     \"x contains non-positive values, so cannot be log-scaled\")\n5165 |             tx = np.log10(tx)\n5166 |         if yscale == 'log':\n5167 |             if np.any(y <= 0.0):\n5168 |                 raise ValueError(\n5169 |                     \"y contains non-positive values, so cannot be log-scaled\")\n5170 |             ty = np.log10(ty)\n5171 |         if extent is not None:\n5172 |             xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax = extent\n5173 |             if xmin > xmax:\n5174 |                 raise ValueError(\"In extent, xmax must be greater than xmin\")\n5175 |             if ymin > ymax:\n5176 |                 raise ValueError(\"In extent, ymax must be greater than ymin\")\n5177 |         else:\n5178 |             xmin, xmax = (tx.min(), tx.max()) if len(x) else (0, 1)\n5179 |             ymin, ymax = (ty.min(), ty.max()) if len(y) else (0, 1)\n5180 | \n5181 |             # to avoid issues with singular data, expand the min/max pairs\n5182 |             xmin, xmax = mtransforms.nonsingular(xmin, xmax, expander=0.1)\n5183 |             ymin, ymax = mtransforms.nonsingular(ymin, ymax, expander=0.1)\n5184 | \n5185 |         nx1 = nx + 1\n5186 |         ny1 = ny + 1\n5187 |         nx2 = nx\n5188 |         ny2 = ny\n5189 |         n = nx1 * ny1 + nx2 * ny2\n5190 | \n5191 |         # In the x-direction, the hexagons exactly cover the region from\n5192 |         # xmin to xmax. Need some padding to avoid roundoff errors.\n5193 |         padding = 1.e-9 * (xmax - xmin)\n5194 |         xmin -= padding\n5195 |         xmax += padding\n5196 |         sx = (xmax - xmin) / nx\n5197 |         sy = (ymax - ymin) / ny\n5198 |         # Positions in hexagon index coordinates.\n5199 |         ix = (tx - xmin) / sx\n5200 |         iy = (ty - ymin) / sy\n5201 |         ix1 = np.round(ix).astype(int)\n5202 |         iy1 = np.round(iy).astype(int)\n5203 |         ix2 = np.floor(ix).astype(int)\n5204 |         iy2 = np.floor(iy).astype(int)\n5205 |         # flat indices, plus one so that out-of-range points go to position 0.\n5206 |         i1 = np.where((0 <= ix1) & (ix1 < nx1) & (0 <= iy1) & (iy1 < ny1),\n5207 |                       ix1 * ny1 + iy1 + 1, 0)\n5208 |         i2 = np.where((0 <= ix2) & (ix2 < nx2) & (0 <= iy2) & (iy2 < ny2),\n5209 |                       ix2 * ny2 + iy2 + 1, 0)\n5210 | \n5211 |         d1 = (ix - ix1) ** 2 + 3.0 * (iy - iy1) ** 2\n5212 |         d2 = (ix - ix2 - 0.5) ** 2 + 3.0 * (iy - iy2 - 0.5) ** 2\n5213 |         bdist = (d1 < d2)\n5214 | \n5215 |         if C is None:  # [1:] drops out-of-range points.\n5216 |             counts1 = np.bincount(i1[bdist], minlength=1 + nx1 * ny1)[1:]\n5217 |             counts2 = np.bincount(i2[~bdist], minlength=1 + nx2 * ny2)[1:]\n5218 |             accum = np.concatenate([counts1, counts2]).astype(float)\n5219 |             if mincnt is not None:\n5220 |                 accum[accum < mincnt] = np.nan\n5221 |             C = np.ones(len(x))\n5222 |         else:\n5223 |             # store the C values in a list per hexagon index\n5224 |             Cs_at_i1 = [[] for _ in range(1 + nx1 * ny1)]\n5225 |             Cs_at_i2 = [[] for _ in range(1 + nx2 * ny2)]\n5226 |             for i in range(len(x)):\n5227 |                 if bdist[i]:\n5228 |                     Cs_at_i1[i1[i]].append(C[i])\n5229 |                 else:\n5230 |                     Cs_at_i2[i2[i]].append(C[i])\n5231 |             if mincnt is None:\n5232 |                 mincnt = 1\n5233 |             accum = np.array(\n5234 |                 [reduce_C_function(acc) if len(acc) >= mincnt else np.nan\n5235 |                  for Cs_at_i in [Cs_at_i1, Cs_at_i2]\n5236 |                  for acc in Cs_at_i[1:]],  # [1:] drops out-of-range points.\n5237 |                 float)\n5238 | \n5239 |         good_idxs = ~np.isnan(accum)\n5240 | \n5241 |         offsets = np.zeros((n, 2), float)\n5242 |         offsets[:nx1 * ny1, 0] = np.repeat(np.arange(nx1), ny1)\n5243 |         offsets[:nx1 * ny1, 1] = np.tile(np.arange(ny1), nx1)\n5244 |         offsets[nx1 * ny1:, 0] = np.repeat(np.arange(nx2) + 0.5, ny2)\n5245 |         offsets[nx1 * ny1:, 1] = np.tile(np.arange(ny2), nx2) + 0.5\n5246 |         offsets[:, 0] *= sx\n5247 |         offsets[:, 1] *= sy\n5248 |         offsets[:, 0] += xmin\n5249 |         offsets[:, 1] += ymin\n5250 |         # remove accumulation bins with no data\n5251 |         offsets = offsets[good_idxs, :]\n5252 |         accum = accum[good_idxs]\n5253 | \n5254 |         polygon = [sx, sy / 3] * np.array(\n5255 |             [[.5, -.5], [.5, .5], [0., 1.], [-.5, .5], [-.5, -.5], [0., -1.]])\n5256 | \n5257 |         if linewidths is None:\n5258 |             linewidths = [mpl.rcParams['patch.linewidth']]\n5259 | \n5260 |         if xscale == 'log' or yscale == 'log':\n5261 |             polygons = np.expand_dims(polygon, 0)\n5262 |             if xscale == 'log':\n5263 |                 polygons[:, :, 0] = 10.0 ** polygons[:, :, 0]\n5264 |                 xmin = 10.0 ** xmin\n5265 |                 xmax = 10.0 ** xmax\n5266 |                 self.set_xscale(xscale)\n5267 |             if yscale == 'log':\n5268 |                 polygons[:, :, 1] = 10.0 ** polygons[:, :, 1]\n5269 |                 ymin = 10.0 ** ymin\n5270 |                 ymax = 10.0 ** ymax\n5271 |                 self.set_yscale(yscale)\n5272 |         else:\n5273 |             polygons = [polygon]\n5274 | \n5275 |         collection = mcoll.PolyCollection(\n5276 |             polygons,\n5277 |             edgecolors=edgecolors,\n5278 |             linewidths=linewidths,\n5279 |             offsets=offsets,\n5280 |             offset_transform=mtransforms.AffineDeltaTransform(self.transData)\n5281 |         )\n5282 | \n5283 |         # Set normalizer if bins is 'log'\n5284 |         if cbook._str_equal(bins, 'log'):\n5285 |             if norm is not None:\n5286 |                 _api.warn_external(\"Only one of 'bins' and 'norm' arguments \"\n5287 |                                    f\"can be supplied, ignoring {bins=}\")\n5288 |             else:\n5289 |                 norm = mcolors.LogNorm(vmin=vmin, vmax=vmax)\n5290 |                 vmin = vmax = None\n5291 |             bins = None\n5292 | \n5293 |         if bins is not None:\n5294 |             if not np.iterable(bins):\n5295 |                 minimum, maximum = min(accum), max(accum)\n5296 |                 bins -= 1  # one less edge than bins\n5297 |                 bins = minimum + (maximum - minimum) * np.arange(bins) / bins\n5298 |             bins = np.sort(bins)\n5299 |             accum = bins.searchsorted(accum)\n5300 | \n5301 |         if colorizer:\n5302 |             collection._set_colorizer_check_keywords(colorizer, cmap=cmap,\n5303 |                                                      norm=norm, vmin=vmin,\n5304 |                                                      vmax=vmax)\n5305 |         else:\n5306 |             collection.set_cmap(cmap)\n5307 |             collection.set_norm(norm)\n5308 |         collection.set_array(accum)\n5309 |         collection.set_alpha(alpha)\n5310 |         collection._internal_update(kwargs)\n5311 |         collection._scale_norm(norm, vmin, vmax)\n5312 | \n5313 |         # autoscale the norm with current accum values if it hasn't been set\n5314 |         if norm is not None:\n5315 |             if collection.norm.vmin is None and collection.norm.vmax is None:\n5316 |                 collection.norm.autoscale()\n5317 | \n5318 |         corners = ((xmin, ymin), (xmax, ymax))\n5319 |         self.update_datalim(corners)\n5320 |         self._request_autoscale_view(tight=True)\n5321 | \n5322 |         # add the collection last\n5323 |         self.add_collection(collection, autolim=False)\n5324 |         if not marginals:\n5325 |             return collection\n5326 | \n5327 |         # Process marginals\n5328 |         bars = []\n5329 |         for zname, z, zmin, zmax, zscale, nbins in [\n5330 |                 (\"x\", x, xmin, xmax, xscale, nx),\n5331 |                 (\"y\", y, ymin, ymax, yscale, 2 * ny),\n5332 |         ]:\n5333 | \n5334 |             if zscale == \"log\":\n5335 |                 bin_edges = np.geomspace(zmin, zmax, nbins + 1)\n5336 |             else:\n5337 |                 bin_edges = np.linspace(zmin, zmax, nbins + 1)\n5338 | \n5339 |             verts = np.empty((nbins, 4, 2))\n5340 |             verts[:, 0, 0] = verts[:, 1, 0] = bin_edges[:-1]\n5341 |             verts[:, 2, 0] = verts[:, 3, 0] = bin_edges[1:]\n5342 |             verts[:, 0, 1] = verts[:, 3, 1] = .00\n5343 |             verts[:, 1, 1] = verts[:, 2, 1] = .05\n5344 |             if zname == \"y\":\n5345 |                 verts = verts[:, :, ::-1]  # Swap x and y.\n5346 | \n5347 |             # Sort z-values into bins defined by bin_edges.\n5348 |             bin_idxs = np.searchsorted(bin_edges, z) - 1\n5349 |             values = np.empty(nbins)\n5350 |             for i in range(nbins):\n5351 |                 # Get C-values for each bin, and compute bin value with\n5352 |                 # reduce_C_function.\n5353 |                 ci = C[bin_idxs == i]\n5354 |                 values[i] = reduce_C_function(ci) if len(ci) > 0 else np.nan\n5355 | \n5356 |             mask = ~np.isnan(values)\n5357 |             verts = verts[mask]\n5358 |             values = values[mask]\n5359 | \n5360 |             trans = getattr(self, f\"get_{zname}axis_transform\")(which=\"grid\")\n5361 |             bar = mcoll.PolyCollection(\n5362 |                 verts, transform=trans, edgecolors=\"face\")\n5363 |             bar.set_array(values)\n5364 |             bar.set_cmap(cmap)\n5365 |             bar.set_norm(norm)\n5366 |             bar.set_alpha(alpha)\n5367 |             bar._internal_update(kwargs)\n5368 |             bars.append(self.add_collection(bar, autolim=False))\n5369 | \n5370 |         collection.hbar, collection.vbar = bars\n5371 | \n5372 |         def on_changed(collection):\n5373 |             collection.hbar.set_cmap(collection.get_cmap())\n5374 |             collection.hbar.set_cmap(collection.get_cmap())\n5375 |             collection.vbar.set_clim(collection.get_clim())\n5376 |             collection.vbar.set_clim(collection.get_clim())\n5377 | \n5378 |         collection.callbacks.connect('changed', on_changed)\n5379 | \n5380 |         return collection\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check for color-related implementations:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['facecolor', 'to_rgba_array']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>lib/matplotlib/**/*.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nlib/matplotlib/backends/backend_pdf.py:RendererPdf.draw_path_collection\nlib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes._parse_scatter_color_args\nlib/matplotlib/tri/_tripcolor.py:tripcolor\nlib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes._parse_scatter_color_args\n##Searching for term \"facecolor\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes._parse_scatter_color_args`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n4539 | def _parse_scatter_color_args(c, edgecolors, kwargs, xsize,\n4540 |                                   get_next_color_func):\n4541 |         \"\"\"\n4542 |         Helper function to process color related arguments of `.Axes.scatter`.\n4543 | \n4544 |         Argument precedence for facecolors:\n4545 | \n4546 |         - c (if not None)\n4547 |         - kwargs['facecolor']\n4548 |         - kwargs['facecolors']\n4549 |         - kwargs['color'] (==kwcolor)\n4550 |         - 'b' if in classic mode else the result of ``get_next_color_func()``\n4551 | \n4552 |         Argument precedence for edgecolors:\n4553 | \n4554 |         - kwargs['edgecolor']\n4555 |         - edgecolors (is an explicit kw argument in scatter())\n4556 |         - kwargs['color'] (==kwcolor)\n4557 |         - 'face' if not in classic mode else None\n4558 | \n4559 |         Parameters\n4560 |         ----------\n4561 |         c : :mpltype:`color` or array-like or list of :mpltype:`color` or None\n4562 |             See argument description of `.Axes.scatter`.\n4563 |         edgecolors : :mpltype:`color` or sequence of color or {'face', 'none'} or None\n4564 |             See argument description of `.Axes.scatter`.\n4565 |         kwargs : dict\n4566 |             Additional kwargs. If these keys exist, we pop and process them:\n4567 |             'facecolors', 'facecolor', 'edgecolor', 'color'\n4568 |             Note: The dict is modified by this function.\n4569 |         xsize : int\n4570 |             The size of the x and y arrays passed to `.Axes.scatter`.\n4571 |         get_next_color_func : callable\n4572 |             A callable that returns a color. This color is used as facecolor\n4573 |             if no other color is provided.\n4574 | \n4575 |             Note, that this is a function rather than a fixed color value to\n4576 |             support conditional evaluation of the next color.  As of the\n4577 |             current implementation obtaining the next color from the\n4578 |             property cycle advances the cycle. This must only happen if we\n4579 |             actually use the color, which will only be decided within this\n4580 |             method.\n4581 | \n4582 |         Returns\n4583 |         -------\n4584 |         c\n4585 |             The input *c* if it was not *None*, else a color derived from the\n4586 |             other inputs or defaults.\n4587 |         colors : array(N, 4) or None\n4588 |             The facecolors as RGBA values, or *None* if a colormap is used.\n4589 |         edgecolors\n4590 |             The edgecolor.\n4591 | \n4592 |         \"\"\"\n4593 |         facecolors = kwargs.pop('facecolors', None)\n4594 |         facecolors = kwargs.pop('facecolor', facecolors)\n4595 |         edgecolors = kwargs.pop('edgecolor', edgecolors)\n4596 | \n4597 |         kwcolor = kwargs.pop('color', None)\n4598 | \n4599 |         if kwcolor is not None and c is not None:\n4600 |             raise ValueError(\"Supply a 'c' argument or a 'color'\"\n4601 |                              \" kwarg but not both; they differ but\"\n4602 |                              \" their functionalities overlap.\")\n4603 | \n4604 |         if kwcolor is not None:\n4605 |             try:\n4606 |                 mcolors.to_rgba_array(kwcolor)\n4607 |             except ValueError as err:\n4608 |                 raise ValueError(\n4609 |                     \"'color' kwarg must be a color or sequence of color \"\n4610 |                     \"specs.  For a sequence of values to be color-mapped, use \"\n4611 |                     \"the 'c' argument instead.\") from err\n4612 |             if edgecolors is None:\n4613 |                 edgecolors = kwcolor\n4614 |             if facecolors is None:\n4615 |                 facecolors = kwcolor\n4616 | \n4617 |         if edgecolors is None and not mpl.rcParams['_internal.classic_mode']:\n4618 |             edgecolors = mpl.rcParams['scatter.edgecolors']\n4619 | \n4620 |         c_was_none = c is None\n4621 |         if c is None:\n4622 |             c = (facecolors if facecolors is not None\n4623 |                  else \"b\" if mpl.rcParams['_internal.classic_mode']\n4624 |                  else get_next_color_func())\n4625 |         c_is_string_or_strings = (\n4626 |             isinstance(c, str)\n4627 |             or (np.iterable(c) and len(c) > 0\n4628 |                 and isinstance(cbook._safe_first_finite(c), str)))\n4629 | \n4630 |         def invalid_shape_exception(csize, xsize):\n4631 |             return ValueError(\n4632 |                 f\"'c' argument has {csize} elements, which is inconsistent \"\n4633 |                 f\"with 'x' and 'y' with size {xsize}.\")\n4634 | \n4635 |         c_is_mapped = False  # Unless proven otherwise below.\n4636 |         valid_shape = True  # Unless proven otherwise below.\n4637 |         if not c_was_none and kwcolor is None and not c_is_string_or_strings:\n4638 |             try:  # First, does 'c' look suitable for value-mapping?\n4639 |                 c = np.asanyarray(c, dtype=float)\n4640 |             except ValueError:\n4641 |                 pass  # Failed to convert to float array; must be color specs.\n4642 |             else:\n4643 |                 # handle the documented special case of a 2D array with 1\n4644 |                 # row which as RGB(A) to broadcast.\n4645 |                 if c.shape == (1, 4) or c.shape == (1, 3):\n4646 |                     c_is_mapped = False\n4647 |                     if c.size != xsize:\n4648 |                         valid_shape = False\n4649 |                 # If c can be either mapped values or an RGB(A) color, prefer\n4650 |                 # the former if shapes match, the latter otherwise.\n4651 |                 elif c.size == xsize:\n4652 |                     c = c.ravel()\n4653 |                     c_is_mapped = True\n4654 |                 else:  # Wrong size; it must not be intended for mapping.\n4655 |                     if c.shape in ((3,), (4,)):\n4656 |                         _api.warn_external(\n4657 |                             \"*c* argument looks like a single numeric RGB or \"\n4658 |                             \"RGBA sequence, which should be avoided as value-\"\n4659 |                             \"mapping will have precedence in case its length \"\n4660 |                             \"matches with *x* & *y*.  Please use the *color* \"\n4661 |                             \"keyword-argument or provide a 2D array \"\n4662 |                             \"with a single row if you intend to specify \"\n4663 |                             \"the same RGB or RGBA value for all points.\")\n4664 |                     valid_shape = False\n4665 |         if not c_is_mapped:\n4666 |             try:  # Is 'c' acceptable as PathCollection facecolors?\n4667 |                 colors = mcolors.to_rgba_array(c)\n4668 |             except (TypeError, ValueError) as err:\n4669 |                 if \"RGBA values should be within 0-1 range\" in str(err):\n4670 |                     raise\n4671 |                 else:\n4672 |                     if not valid_shape:\n4673 |                         raise invalid_shape_exception(c.size, xsize) from err\n4674 |                     # Both the mapping *and* the RGBA conversion failed: pretty\n4675 |                     # severe failure => one may appreciate a verbose feedback.\n4676 |                     raise ValueError(\n4677 |                         f\"'c' argument must be a color, a sequence of colors, \"\n4678 |                         f\"or a sequence of numbers,